              "title": "Result",
              "type": "string"
            }
          },
          "required": [
            "result"
          ],
          "title": "_my_ticketsOutput",
          "type": "object"
        }
      },
      {
        "name": "search",
        "description": "Search SeatGeek events, optionally with their ticket listings. This is the first step for any query that names a team / artist / venue / city / date / category.\n\nPick `detail` — this is the one decision that matters:\n- `detail='events'` (default): event summaries only. Use to find, identify, or list events ('when do the Mets play', 'comedy shows in NYC', 'is X on tour').\n- `detail='events_with_listings'`: events PLUS their ticket inventory/pricing. Use whenever the user asks about price, availability, cheapest, best seats, a budget, or specific sections/seats. One call answers the whole question — do NOT call twice. The result summarises the FULL matched set: every distinct section (with counts), the seat features present, a per-event breakdown, and the overall price range — so you can read the exact section names / seat-feature ids off the output and refine without a second discovery call.\n\nAnchors (pass at least one; combine freely): `event_ids` when you already know the exact event(s) (e.g. from a prior `detail='events'` search) — pass one or several to lock EVERY mode to just those events and drill into their listings, sections, and pricing; it overrides the performer/venue/date filters (they're ignored when `event_ids` is set); `performer_ids` when the id(s) are already known (from pre-fetched context or a prior result) — pass SEVERAL for a matchup ('Mets vs Dodgers' → both ids), never concatenate names into one `performer_query`. For a matchup ALSO set `performer_match='all'` to return only the head-to-head games (`performer_match` defaults to 'any', which matches events with EITHER performer, i.e. every game of either team). `performer_query` ('Mets') for a single free-text name; or `venue_query` ('Citi Field'); `taxonomy` (pick the most specific value — 'mlb' < 'baseball' < 'sports' — allowed values are in the `taxonomy` schema); `taxonomy_ids` (list of category ids) when a category id is already known (e.g. from pre-fetched context) — it covers the FULL taxonomy space, unlike the curated `taxonomy` slug list; pass it to filter by a resolved category, and never retype a category word into `performer_query`; `main_event_ids` (parent event id(s)) to fetch an event's PARKING and add-on passes — pair it with `detail='events_with_listings'` to surface the parking listings for that event; `genre_slugs` for concert genres; to anchor by place pass `location_query` (a bare city or place name, e.g. 'Brooklyn', 'Austin') — it is geocoded for you, so do NOT call geocode_location first. Pass `location_coordinates` ('lat,lon' string, or a [lat, lon] pair) instead only when you already have coordinates (the user's own 'near me' location, or a prior geocode); if both are passed, coordinates win. Either pairs with optional `radius_miles`. The result echoes the place a `location_query` resolved to — if it's the wrong city, retry with the parent city name.\n\nRefinements (any `detail`): `performer_role` ('any' default — home AND away games / every appearance; narrow to `'home_team'` ONLY when the user says home ('Mets home games', 'when are they at home'), `'away_team'` when they say away/road. A team question with no home/away wording keeps the default: a fan asking about 'the Commanders-Titans game' means the game, whichever stadium it is at), `datetime_local_start`/`datetime_local_end` (ISO 8601 local), `day_of_week` (1=Mon…7=Sun; [6,7]=weekend), `day_night`, `season_stages`, `include_past_events`. Apply ONLY the filters the user actually stated — e.g. 'day games' sets `day_night='day'` alone; add `day_of_week` only when they name days or say 'weekend'/'weekday'. Don't narrow by something they didn't ask for.\n\nListings-only refinements (apply when `detail='events_with_listings'`): `sections`, `seat_feature_ids`, `min_price_cents`/`max_price_cents`, `min_deal_quality`, `min_quantity`, `delivery_methods`, `accessible_only`, `exclude_obstructed`, `exclude_partial_view`, `general_admission`. `sections` and `seat_feature_ids` match EXACT values — never invent them. To target a tier the user names loosely (e.g. 'club', 'field level', 'bleachers'), do a first `detail='events_with_listings'` call WITHOUT `sections`, read the real section names + seat-feature ids from the result's summary, then call again with those exact values.\n\nSort: events default `popularity`; listings default `deal_quality` descending — a higher deal quality means better value, so the best-value listings come first. Use `datetime` for schedules, `price`/`lowest_price` for cheapest-first.\n\nPaging: results are stably ordered and the header reports the full match count plus a per-event breakdown and overall price range (computed across ALL matches, not just the shown page). Rows show `per_page` at a time (listings default 30, events 15; max 50); when the header says more are available, request the next page with `page=2`, `page=3`, … keeping the same filters. (`per_page` applies to both modes; the legacy `limit` still works for listings but is deprecated.)",
